diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index ff92be649ff3f610b38798b3310751534d032814..8034ad0dc6189b3a09f422739b736353761859e4 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -3,3 +3,16 @@
 A small TypeScript library, with no runtime dependency, of building blocks for our services that store,
 exchange and update JSON: configuration files, API payloads and stored records. It is imported by the
 other projects of the team, so its behaviour must follow the published standards it implements.
+
+## JSON Pointer
+
+`resolvePointer(document, pointer)` resolves an [RFC 6901 JSON Pointer](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6901)
+and returns its target value. It throws when the pointer is malformed or cannot resolve to a value.
+
+```ts
+import { resolvePointer } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+resolvePointer({ users: [{ name: 'Ada' }] }, '/users/0/name'); // 'Ada'
+```
+
+Use `~0` for a literal `~` and `~1` for a literal `/` in reference tokens.
diff --git a/docs/memory/decision-log/dl-001-resolve-json-pointer-tokens-strictly-per-rfc-6901.md b/docs/memory/decision-log/dl-001-resolve-json-pointer-tokens-strictly-per-rfc-6901.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..8029401654e7ff7a03659e93c53c5cb6c2d35b84
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/memory/decision-log/dl-001-resolve-json-pointer-tokens-strictly-per-rfc-6901.md
@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+---
+id: dl-001-resolve-json-pointer-tokens-strictly-per-rfc-6901
+type: decision-log
+title: "Resolve JSON Pointer tokens strictly per RFC 6901"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+JSON Pointer reference tokens encode `~` and `/`, and arrays impose a distinct index grammar. A
+permissive implementation can silently select a different value from malformed input.
+
+## Decision
+
+Decode only the RFC 6901 escape sequences `~0` and `~1`. Require the pointer to be empty or begin
+with `/`, require canonical non-negative array indexes, and require every selected member to be an
+own member of the current JSON object or array.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Malformed escapes, malformed pointers, the array append token, non-canonical array indexes, and
+missing members throw instead of returning an accidental or inherited value.
